// Canonical Swift resilience package requirements (#1146).
//
// This is a Package.swift FRAGMENT, not a manifest — the Swift analogue of the Go
// payload's go.mod.deps and the Java payload's build.gradle.kts dependency block.
// Unlike every sibling, it carries NOTHING TO PASTE.
//
// NO THIRD-PARTY DEPENDENCY, deliberately. The circuit breaker is the payload-owned
// `CircuitBreaker` actor in DependencyCatalog.swift, and the bounded jittered retry is
// a loop beside it — the Go payload set the precedent with its retry half, about
// thirty lines of stdlib rather than a second blessed library. No maintained Swift
// breaker passed the fit-check: README.md, "Why a payload-owned actor breaker",
// scores every candidate on the six required properties and records why each was
// rejected. Everything this payload imports ships with the toolchain: Foundation
// (plus FoundationNetworking on Linux, for the worked example's URLSession) and the
// ops-api payload's own types, which it reads from the SAME target.
//
// Bootstrap adds DEPENDENCIES ONLY and performs no `targets:` edit, and this payload
// needs neither: it sits in the service's own target beside `Ops/OpsApi.swift`, at
// `Sources/<ServiceTarget>/Resilience/`. The ops-api payload's Package.swift.deps is
// still pasted in full — that is the half with packages in it.
//
// THE MANIFEST MUST DECLARE `// swift-tools-version:6.1` OR NEWER, on its very first
// line — the same floor as the ops-api payload it shares a target with. The payload is
// written for the Swift 6 language mode and compiles with zero concurrency diagnostics
// under it (the breaker is an actor, every value crossing it is Sendable, and the
// declaration parser uses typed throws); below 6.1 the tools version cannot express
// `.swiftLanguageMode(.v6)` as a per-target setting, so the strict checking it is
// verified against silently does not happen.
//
// And on the target that holds Ops/ and Resilience/, so the payload is checked the way
// it was verified — an adopter-owned `targets:` edit, never bootstrap's:
//
//     swiftSettings: [.swiftLanguageMode(.v6)]
//
// Platforms floor: none beyond the ops-api payload's `.macOS(.v13), .iOS(.v16)`.
// Linux has no platform floor to state and is the deployment target.
